title: Key levels
tags: Keybindings [[Key usage]]
kind: reference
summary: Every key the shipped keybindings may use, by level — what it costs to type —, and for level 0, the 26 letters alone, which are used and which are left.

A key is not worth another: a letter under the fingers costs less than a
Ctrl chord, and there are only 26 of them. The ''level'' of a key says what it costs
to type, on bépo, AZERTY and QWERTY alike ([[Choosing keybindings]]); the lower, the
more precious. A test holds every shipped key to one of these levels
(`test_shipped_keys_follow_the_rules_of_choice`, through `key_level` in
`keybindings.rs`).

|!Level |!Keys |
|0 |a letter alone: `a` … `z` |
|1 |another key typed without a modifier: `,` `-` `=`, the named keys — arrows, Enter, Escape, Backspace, Delete, Insert, Tab, Space, Home, End, PageUp, PageDown, Menu, `plus` — and F1 … F12 |
|2 |one Shift: Shift and a key of level 0 or 1; and alone, a character that needs Shift on one of the three layouts — the digits and `. / ; : ! * ( ) " $ % ?` |
|3 |Ctrl or Alt, with or without Shift, on a key of level 0 or 1 |

How many keys each level has, and how many the shipped file uses as a first key, is
counted below.

* What has ''no level'' is never shipped: a key that needs AltGr (`[ ] { } \ | ~ ^ # @ & _ < >`), a dead key, an accented letter, and a level 2 character under Ctrl or Alt — the Shift a layout needs changes the character, so `ctrl+.` cannot be typed on AZERTY.
* A ''sequence'' takes from the keyboard only its first key: `g l` spends `g`, and the second letters are free again in every domain. That is why the level of a binding is its first key's, and why level 0 is counted by first keys.

!! Level 0

A letter alone is spent only on what is done all the time — the class A commands
([[Command classes]]) — or given to a ''domain'', whose keys all start with it, or to
a ''target'' prefix (`i` here, `b` beside, `n` new). A letter used by nothing is in
reserve, for a domain to come. The list below is generated from the shipped
`keybindings.toml`: a letter whose first key is bound in one panel only is used there,
and named with it.
